{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"body":"## PROBE-4B9E-R1: the probe ran, FAILED AT SETUP, and destroyed its own evidence\n\nRun 30930839323, `main` @ 40a0efad8. **No verdict recorded. The blocking unknown is still open, so\nthe Rank 1 cell stays unwritten** — which is the discipline this ticket set for itself.\n\n### What the run said, in full\n\n```\nPROBE SETUP FAILED: Epic Link=None Epic Name=None\n##[error]Process completed with exit code 1.\n##[error]No files were found with the provided path: .../jira-dc-epic-link-probe\n```\n\nThat is the entire diagnostic surface. Jira booted (the step started ~10 min into the run and\nanswered in 1.4s), so this is not a readiness timeout.\n\n### The defect that made it undiagnosable — and the more useful finding\n\nEvidence was written only AFTER the last question, and every `PROBE SETUP FAILED` path raises\n`SystemExit`. So the one artifact built to explain a failure was the one artifact guaranteed to be\ndiscarded exactly when it was needed, and the upload step then failed for having nothing to upload.\n\n**On an amd64-only image every attempt costs a full boot cycle, so an undiagnosable failure is the\nexpensive failure.** I built the probe to answer a question and did not build it to survive not\nanswering it. Fixed in Gerrit 1373:\n\n* evidence is written on ANY exit — clean, `SystemExit`, or unexpected exception;\n* discovery reports WHY: the HTTP status and body type when `/rest/api/2/field` is unusable, or the\n  inventory size plus the names actually present when the field is genuinely absent. A bare `None`\n  conflated \"instance unreachable/unauthorised\" with \"field does not exist\";\n* `Accept: application/json` is now sent, matching `conftest._request` and\n  `test_dc_mutations._admin_request`. The probe sent only `Content-Type`, and it is the one caller\n  in this family that omitted `Accept` — a plausible cause of a non-list body, though NOT yet\n  demonstrated as the cause, and I am not recording a diagnosis I have not measured.\n\n### Why the field is probably not actually missing\n\n`_named_field_id` in the live mutation suite resolves \"Epic Link\" against the same image by the same\nname-match over `/rest/api/2/field`, and the capability map measured WRITING the Epic Link\n(req-0040/0041). So an inventory genuinely lacking the field would contradict two prior\nmeasurements. That is a reason to instrument rather than a reason to conclude — the next run's\nevidence decides it.\n\n### CONSTRAINT 1, REFINED — worth carrying forward\n\nMy earlier note said a new `workflow_dispatch` workflow \"cannot be dispatched until it lands\". The\nprecise rule is narrower and more useful: the workflow FILE must exist on the DEFAULT branch to be\ndispatchable at all, but once it does, `--ref` may target any ref that GitHub can see, and the run\nthen executes THAT ref's workflow and scripts. So the landing cost is paid once per workflow, not\nonce per iteration of the script it runs."},"env_id":"ade0f2ef-51cc-45a2-bb97-c09846cd3df5","event_type":"COMMENT","timestamp":1785863727782850001,"uuid":"7131ed2f-e67e-4410-a719-6822741a4d3e"}